    "q-161691": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161691",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/b0645201b3726750226811e0c714dc09d3075021fcbb6ddd1525748e9539c02d.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/c4cf12ec9b53282620c1730f824c96d98e0b9dbabee0478bf258cf6c2741da81.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/b497e6ef73b5b16e11baa08819e9628d3b5e1f2ee4d05fbafec48f73e087cbf1.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=1+y+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+y+z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(1+y)z+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}+y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
