      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/f7fe261b981f8c97a123675d1385237d62cdbb8c9976dc8c3f1841f073e497de.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/ca8ce5a3ed7f3f164b348268cd0805e05df1e0ad8bf3381ca6368421ffe380cc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-y+2z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/52f67235db14b903955660728642ed6b00603a12f480501767a98058f47d157a.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-y+2z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y+2z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2-y+2z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-y+2z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2-y+2z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(2-y)z+{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((2-y)(3)+{3}^{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15-3y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[15y-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(30-6)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
