      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/4f1ccfc3bdde9872114cc10c333f4d5dfcb1a271e97a3e37a58eefcb7eff1a48.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/a90ab6eea35b1290e49106ac96c98241f637173c079812048feea9b25d911971.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+2y-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117154/assets/b497e6ef73b5b16e11baa08819e9628d3b5e1f2ee4d05fbafec48f73e087cbf1.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+2y-z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+2y-z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2+2y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2y-z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2y-z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(2+2y)z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}+2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}y+{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
