    "q-38895": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38895",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $xy$-plane, the surface $z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and the planes $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ $y=0,$ $y=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
